1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,560
Hi. In this video, we'll look at how you can use Burp to determine a session timeout.

2
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:17,280
When a user doesn't use an application for a certain amount of time, most applications will

3
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:23,280
automatically log out the user and destroy their session. To determine how long it takes for a

4
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:27,760
session to timeout, you can use Burp Intruder to issue the same request multiple times with

5
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:33,200
increasing delays. This enables you to test compliance with security standards that require

6
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:39,280
applications to timeout within a specified period. A longer timeout gives an attacker more time to use

7
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:45,440
or guess a session token. If you'd like to follow along with this process, you can use ginandjuice.shop,

8
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:51,680
our deliberately vulnerable demonstration site. First of all, in Burp's browser, log into your

9
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:57,600
target website. If you're using ginandjuice.shop, the correct credentials are Carlos and

10
00:00:57,600 --> 00:01:05,760
hunter2. Go to the proxy http history tab in Burp and identify a logged in request.

11
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:18,080
Right click and choose send to intruder. Now go to the intruder tab and we can set up our attack.

12
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:27,280
Go to the intruder payloads tab. Under payload type, select null payloads. Under payload settings,

13
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:37,200
select continue indefinitely. Now go to the resource pool tab. Select create new resource pool.

14
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:51,120
In resource pool settings, select delay between requests. Then choose to increase the delay in

15
00:01:51,120 --> 00:02:01,520
increments of x milliseconds. Add a delay time. For this test, we'll specify 300 000 milliseconds, so 5 minutes.

16
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:16,240
Click start attack. Intruder sends each request, increasing the delay between each request by the specified amount.

17
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:23,920
With the attack results dialog open, click columns on the top level menu and select time of day.

18
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:30,480
A time of day column is added to the results table. You can now sort the results by this column.

19
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:35,280
Review the responses as the attack progresses.

20
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:39,840
Identify the first request that indicates the session is invalid.

21
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:44,080
For example, look for a redirection maybe to a login page.

22
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:53,920
To determine the session timeout, identify the time difference between the logged out request and the

23
00:02:53,920 --> 00:03:00,240
previous request. In this example, we can see there was a 5 minute gap between each request,

24
00:03:00,240 --> 00:03:03,040
and the timeout occurred between 10 and 15 minutes.

25
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:08,720
The second request is to be added to the session timeout.

26
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:11,840
The second request is to be added to the session timeout.

27
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:13,840
The second request is to be added to the session timeout.

28
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:15,840
The second request is to be added to the session timeout.

29
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:17,840
The second request is to be added to the session timeout.

30
